steps.body:
Without `break`, Java does not stop at the end of a case. It *falls through* and runs the next case's code too, whatever its label says. A forgotten `break` is the classic switch bug.

Fall-through is also how you group values: stack several labels over one block.

```java
switch (day) {
    case "Saturday":
    case "Sunday":
        System.out.println("Weekend");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Weekday");
}
```

A `String` switch compares with `equals`, so it matches contents and is case-sensitive: `"saturday"` would not match. Switching on a `null` String throws a `NullPointerException`.

A `char` switch uses single-quoted labels:

```java
switch (grade) {
    case 'A':
    case 'B':
        result = "Excellent";
        break;
    default:
        result = "Other";
}
```

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String day = "Saturday";
        String kind;
        switch (day) {
            case "Saturday":
            case "Sunday":
                kind = "Weekend";
                break;
            case "Monday":
            case "Tuesday":
            case "Wednesday":
            case "Thursday":
            case "Friday":
                kind = "Weekday";
                break;
            default:
                kind = "Not a day";
        }
        System.out.println(day + ": " + kind);

        day = "Friday";
        switch (day) {
            case "Friday":
                System.out.println("Friday: last workday");
                // no break here, so Java keeps running the next case
            case "Saturday":
                System.out.println("Saturday: weekend starts");
                break;
            default:
                System.out.println("Some other day");
        }
    }
}

