steps.body:
Java 14 added arrow labels. `case X ->` runs only the code on its right: there is no fall-through, so you never write `break`. List several values after one `case`, separated by commas.

```java
switch (day) {
    case 1, 7 -> System.out.println("Weekend");
    case 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 -> System.out.println("Weekday");
    default -> System.out.println("Not a day");
}
```

The right side is a single expression, such as a `println` call, or a block in braces when a case needs more. A statement like `if` must go inside a block.

```java
case 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 -> {
    System.out.println("Weekday");
    System.out.println("Alarm on");
}
```

Prefer arrows in new code. They are shorter and remove the missing `break` bug. One switch must use either all arrows or all colons: mixing them is a compile error.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int day = 1;
        switch (day) {
            case 1, 7 -> System.out.println("Day " + day + ": Weekend");
            case 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 -> {
                System.out.println("Day " + day + ": Weekday");
                System.out.println("Alarm set for 7:00");
            }
            default -> System.out.println("Day " + day + ": Not a day");
        }

        day = 4;
        switch (day) {
            case 1, 7 -> System.out.println("Day " + day + ": Weekend");
            case 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 -> {
                System.out.println("Day " + day + ": Weekday");
                System.out.println("Alarm set for 7:00");
            }
            default -> System.out.println("Day " + day + ": Not a day");
        }
    }
}

