steps.body:
A switch can also *be* a value. Put it on the right of `=` and each arrow gives the result. The whole statement ends with `};` because it is an assignment.

```java
int days = switch (month) {
    case 4, 6, 9, 11 -> 30;
    case 2 -> 28;
    default -> 31;
};
```

When a case needs more than one line, use a block and hand back the value with `yield`. `yield` is the only way out of that block: `break` there is a compile error.

```java
case 2 -> {
    if (isLeap) {
        yield 29;
    } else {
        yield 28;
    }
}
```

A switch expression must be exhaustive: every possible value needs a result. For `int` and `String` that means a `default`, or the code does not compile. Every path through a block must reach a `yield`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int year = 2024;
        boolean isLeap = year % 4 == 0 && (year % 100 != 0 || year % 400 == 0);

        int month = 4;
        int days = switch (month) {
            case 1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12 -> 31;
            case 4, 6, 9, 11 -> 30;
            case 2 -> {
                if (isLeap) {
                    yield 29;
                } else {
                    yield 28;
                }
            }
            default -> 0;
        };
        System.out.println("Month " + month + " has " + days + " days");

        month = 2;
        days = switch (month) {
            case 1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12 -> 31;
            case 4, 6, 9, 11 -> 30;
            case 2 -> {
                if (isLeap) {
                    yield 29;
                } else {
                    yield 28;
                }
            }
            default -> 0;
        };
        System.out.println("Month " + month + " has " + days + " days");
    }
}

